Fix a one-byte stack overflow when a card line fills the buffer.

load_line() (misc.c) fills a caller's buffer with

    while( num_chr < LINE_LEN )
      buff[num_chr++] = (char)chr;

and then terminates it with

    buff[num_chr] = '\0';

When the line is at least LINE_LEN characters long the loop leaves num_chr
equal to LINE_LEN, so the terminator is written at buff[LINE_LEN] -- one past
the end of a buffer declared char[LINE_LEN].

Confirmed with AddressSanitizer against v1.3.2, reading a deck whose second CM
card is 140 characters:

    ERROR: AddressSanitizer: stack-buffer-overflow
      #0 load_line misc.c:172
      #1 main      main.c:285
    [2160, 2292) 'line_buf' <== Memory access at offset 2292 overflows this

It does not usually crash: main()'s infile[81] and otfile[81] sit next to
line_buf, so the stray byte lands in one of those rather than on the canary.

Giving the buffer one more byte is the smaller change, and keeps load_line's
contract ("fills up to LINE_LEN characters, then terminates") intact for every
other caller. Fixing it in load_line instead would silently shorten every
line it reads.

Reported upstream; carried here until it is released.

--- a/main.c
+++ b/main.c
@@ -38,7 +38,7 @@
 int main( int argc, char **argv )
 {
   char infile[81] = "", otfile[81] = "";
-  char ain[3], line_buf[LINE_LEN];
+  char ain[3], line_buf[LINE_LEN+1];

 /* input card mnemonic list */
 #define NUM_CMNDS  20
